T1  - Separating intent from execution: A defense-in-depth security architecture for LLM-based multi-agent systems
AU  - Chahine, Khaled
JO  - Expert Systems with Applications
VL  - 332
SP  - 133781
PY  - 2027
DA  - 2027/01/01/
SN  - 0957-4174
DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2026.133781
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0957417426026898
KW  - Multi-agent systems
KW  - Large language models
KW  - Decentralized identifiers
KW  - Verifiable credentials
KW  - Security architecture
KW  - Zero trust
KW  - Threat modeling
AB  - Autonomous multi-agent systems require secure identity management, yet delegating security-critical operations to probabilistic language models introduces fundamental reliability risks. Recent architectural frameworks for agentic AI identity management provide comprehensive designs leveraging Decentralized Identifiers (DIDs), Verifiable Credentials (VCs), and cross-protocol session management. However, empirical evaluation reveals that when Large Language Models (LLMs) directly orchestrate these security protocols, completion rates collapse to as low as 4%, security-critical failures occur, and latency becomes prohibitive. This paper proposes a formal architectural separation between an “Intent Layer” where LLMs operate non-deterministically and a “Security Execution Layer” (SEL) where cryptographic operations execute deterministically. It presents a threat model that enumerates adversary capabilities, defines the interface contract with verifiable invariants aligned with DIF Presentation Exchange and DIDComm v2, integrates privacy-preserving credential features, including selective disclosure, and proposes defense-in-depth mechanisms for high-risk authorization decisions. To validate the architecture, a prototype simulator implementing 23 experiments across 16 attack vectors is evaluated against three live LLM APIs: Google Gemini 2.5 Flash, Anthropic Claude Sonnet 4, and OpenAI GPT-4o-mini. The evaluation compares five architectures: the SEL, an LLM-only raw baseline, a pre-parsed baseline, an LLM with function-calling/structured output, and a pure deterministic rule engine. Empirical results demonstrate that the SEL achieves 99% correctness with zero injection bypass across all three providers, compared to 0–80% for LLM baselines. The rule engine matches the SEL on structured-claim scenarios but cannot handle credentials with vague capability labels or unstructured endorsements; the SEL correctly interprets these on two of three providers, establishing that LLM-based interpretation adds genuine capability beyond what deterministic systems can provide. Defense-in-depth is empirically validated: GPT-4o-mini’s Step 3 is fooled by 66.7% of indirect injection attempts, whereas the deterministic Step 4 policy gate catches 100% of these failures, yielding a measured 66.7-percentage-point safety margin.
