AB  - The increasing complexity of organizations' information systems (IS) reinforces the importance of Enterprise Architecture (EA) as a Knowledge Management (KM) discipline. However, current EA practices often struggle to externalize and operationalize architectural knowledge, leaving it fragmented across multiple static models, undocumented flows, and expert silos. This study aims to demonstrate how artificial intelligence (AI)-driven observability can support the externalization, validation, and reuse of architectural knowledge. Our approach uniquely integrates EA, KM, and AI into a single operationalized Design Science Research (DSR) artifact. To address this question, we use the DSR methodology, which allows us to develop a modular software artifact integrating large language models, semantic search, graph-based reasoning, and log analysis to improve architectural understanding. The different modules of this artifact enable natural language interaction with architectural knowledge, real-time deviation detection, decision traceability, and interactive visualization. Following an expert panel evaluation based on concrete use cases, our artifact demonstrated promising evidence and high perceived utility in governance, compliance, and collaboration in organizations' IS. The study provides (1) a reconceptualization of observability as a knowledge development process in EA, (2) a validated artifact operationalizing this vision, and (3) empirical insights into AI-supported epistemic practices in digital architecture governance. We conclude that observability, enhanced by AI, constitutes a strategic lever to transform EA into a living knowledge infrastructure.
