AB  - Cloud-based Digital Twins (DTs) represent a transformative approach to modernizing conventional power systems into intelligent smart grids through incremental, data-driven advancements. Serving as scalable, real-time digital replicas of physical grid assets and operations, DTs enable enhanced monitoring, analytics, and control capabilities across the grid infrastructure. This chapter offers a thorough study of the technical underpinnings, integration techniques, data architecture, and cybersecurity systems supporting the deployment of DTs in modern power systems. It begins with a comparative analysis of cloud computing models, centralized, edge, and hybrid, alongside service paradigms including infrastructure as a service , platform as a service, and software as a service, supported by functionalities from well-known providers including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud. Emphasizing interoperability standards like the open platform communications unified architecture and the International Electrotechnical Commission common information model to allow seamless communication, the study focuses on the integration of DTs with essential grid management systems, including supervisory control and data acquisition, energy management system, and distributed energy resource management system. Cloud-native data storage and processing approaches, including time-series databases, data lakes, and graph structures, are assessed in relation to real-time analytics, load forecasting, fault detection, and predictive maintenance. A multitiered cybersecurity architecture, including infrastructure-level protections, effective cryptographic techniques, secure application programming interfaces, and compliance-oriented procedures, addresses security issues. The evolving risk landscape of smart grid systems is examined, stressing mitigation techniques aligned with the shared responsibility model in cloud contexts. By means of intelligent control, real-time data processing, and secure interoperability between cloud and edge platforms, cloud-hosted DTs present a revolutionary basis for utilities to manage dispersed energy resources, optimize operations, and adapt to dynamic grid demands. These advances help to create more flexible, sustainable, and resilient power systems equipped to support the energy transformation.
