KW  - Threat taxonomy
KW  - Cyber–Physical systems
KW  - Safety-security convergence
KW  - Embodied AI security
AB  - The proliferation of robotic Cyber–Physical Systems (CPS) introduces critical security risks, as digital vulnerabilities can be directly exploited to inflict kinetic harm. Effectively mitigating these threats requires systematically analyzing the robotic architecture. However, existing studies primarily focus on hardware constraints, network protocols, or software logic in isolation, lacking a unified architectural perspective on robotic cybersecurity. To holistically capture the cyber–physical interdependencies between physical constraints and software attack surfaces, this paper proposes the Architecture-Driven Hexa-Layer (ADHL) taxonomy. This unified framework categorizes the modern threat landscape and corresponding defense strategies across six distinct dimensions: the Device, Network, Control, Data, Algorithm, and Application layers. Based on the ADHL framework, this paper analyzes attack vectors ranging from low-level sensor spoofing to emerging semantic manipulations in Embodied Artificial Intelligence (AI), and synthesizes corresponding mitigation mechanisms. Furthermore, this paper quantitatively investigates the performance-security paradox, highlighting the inherent trade-off between real-time control requirements and security-induced latency. The review concludes by identifying pivotal challenges in achieving safety-security convergence and outlining future research directions, including lightweight cryptographic co-design, physics-informed digital twins, and neuromorphic edge security.
